To **stable sort** in JavaScript (i.e., sort elements while preserving their relative order if they compare as equal), you don't have to do anything special in modern JavaScript engines. As of **ECMAScript 2019** (ES10), [`Array.prototype.sort()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/sort) is **guaranteed to be stable** in all modern browsers and Node.js (v12+).

### Example:

```js
const arr = [
  {name: 'apple', count: 2},
  {name: 'banana', count: 3},
  {name: 'carrot', count: 2},
];

arr.sort((a, b) => a.count - b.count);

console.log(arr);
// Output is:
// [
//   {name: 'apple', count: 2},
//   {name: 'carrot', count: 2},  // <-- preserves original order for equal 'count'
//   {name: 'banana', count: 3},
// ]
```

Here, both `'apple'` and `'carrot'` have the same `count` value. The sorted order **respects their original order**, thanks to the stable sort guarantee.

---

## What If I Need to Support Older Environments?
If you are targeting very old JavaScript environments (ES8/ES9, or very old browsers) where `.sort()` *may* be unstable, you can **decorate-sort-undecorate (Schwartzian transform)** to enforce stability:

```js
const arr = [
  {name: 'apple', count: 2},
  {name: 'banana', count: 3},
  {name: 'carrot', count: 2},
];

// 1. Decorate with original index
const decorated = arr.map((item, idx) => ({item, idx}));

// 2. Sort with stability hack
decorated.sort((a, b) => {
  const diff = a.item.count - b.item.count;
  return diff !== 0 ? diff : a.idx - b.idx;
});

// 3. Undecorate
const result = decorated.map(entry => entry.item);

console.log(result);
// The result is stably sorted!
```

---

## TL;DR

- **Modern JavaScript:** Native `.sort()` is stable, just use it.
- **Old JavaScript:** Use a decorate/sort/undecorate (DSU) pattern to enforce stability.

---

**References:**
- [MDN: Array.prototype.sort() – Stability](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/sort#stability)
- [ECMAScript® 2019 Language Specification | Array.prototype.sort()](https://262.ecma-international.org/10.0/#sec-array.prototype.sort)

