Sure! Bit twiddling is a common way to enumerate all subsets (the power set) when the set size is small (\(< 20\)). For a string `s` of length \(n\), each subset corresponds to a bit pattern of \(n\) bits, where the \(i\)-th bit is `1` if the \(i\)-th character is present.

## Example code (C++)

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

void enumerate_subsets(const std::string& s) {
    int n = s.length();
    int total = 1 << n; // 2^n subsets
    for (int mask = 0; mask < total; ++mask) {
        std::string subset;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            if (mask & (1 << i)) {
                subset += s[i];
            }
        }
        std::cout << subset << '\n';
    }
}

int main() {
    std::string s = "abc";
    enumerate_subsets(s);
}
```

